 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 54,
   "y": 32,
   "text": "TRYFAN, 917 m. ADAM AND EVE, the two stones on top. Jumping between them is traditional. Landing is optional."
  },
  {
   "x": 66,
   "y": 18,
   "text": "LLYN OGWEN. Cold, deep and in no mood to be swum. Deep water ahead needs a brook-wader."
  },
  {
   "x": 35,
   "y": 8,
   "text": "NANT FFRANCON. A glacier carved this valley, then left. Mind the steps (all the steps)."
  },
  {
   "x": 37,
   "y": 22,
   "text": "SCREE SHORTCUT. Loose rock, short cut, long fall. Fell-scramble only. (Rhybudd: llithrig.)"
  },
  {
   "x": 42,
   "y": 4,
   "text": "PEN YR OLE WEN. 'Top of the white slope.' Welsh place names are honest."
  },
  {
   "x": 3,
   "y": 17,
   "text": "WEST: Llanberis and Yr Wyddfa. Gorllewin. Pack a coat; Cymru has several seasons per hour."
  },
  {
   "x": 69,
   "y": 17,
   "text": "EAST: Llangollen and the Dee. Dwyrain. Yes, there's a lot of up in between."
  },
  {
   "x": 21,
   "y": 21,
   "text": "BETHESDA SLATE TIPS. The Penrhyn quarry roofed half the world. Please do not take a roof home."
  },
  {
   "x": 58,
   "y": 24,
   "text": "TRYFAN NORTH FACE. No path, no handrail, and no apologies. Hands required (use them)."
  },
  {
   "x": 21,
   "y": 18,
   "plaque": "ogwen_penrhyn_lockout",
   "title": "The Penrhyn Lockout",
   "text": "From 1900 to 1903 the quarrymen of Bethesda were locked out of Penrhyn Quarry in a bitter fight over pay and their right to a union. It was one of the longest industrial disputes in British history. Cards in many windows read 'Nid oes bradwr yn y tŷ hwn': there is no traitor in this house."
  },
  {
   "x": 10,
   "y": 21,
   "plaque": "ogwen_snowdon_lily",
   "title": "Snowdon Lily",
   "text": "The Snowdon lily, Lili'r Wyddfa, is a tiny white flower left over from the ice age. In Britain it grows only on a few cold, high crags in Eryri, including those around Cwm Idwal. It was first recorded by the Welsh naturalist Edward Lhuyd, hence its old Latin name, Lloydia."
  },
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 9,
   "plaque": "landsky_idwal",
   "title": "Cwm Idwal",
   "text": "Cwm Idwal was Wales's first National Nature Reserve, in 1954. Its cliffs are folded volcanic ash, bent into a great U that geologists call the Idwal syncline, and the heaps of rubble by the lake are moraines, dumped by the glacier that scooped the hollow. Charles Darwin came here in 1831 with his geology teacher and saw none of it; he came back in 1842, after reading about glaciers, and wrote that a house burnt down by fire could not tell its story more plainly."
  }
 ],
